WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.840
POLYMATTER: Give each country a blank canvas with no rules,

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:05.520
and you can expect the results to be-- unique.

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:08.700
But strangely, national flags are pretty similar.

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:11.060
44 have three horizontal stripes,

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:13.820
10 have one five pointed star on the left side,

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:16.709
and only eleven flags aren't red or blue.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:19.580
And sure, maybe these are just popular colors,
(38% of people like blue and 11% of people like red)

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:22.220
but then where is the much-loved purple?

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:25.280
The necessary coloring wasn't always easy to come by.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:30.960
But it's 2016, and this is still the most purple flag; you've got to be kidding me.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.240
Of course there are a few oddball flags,

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:36.560
but they're mostly different for the same reason-- weird shapes.

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:40.240
So let's settle this; why are flags so similar?

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:41.860
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:44.160
POLYMATTER: The idea of a flag is to reflect its country;

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:47.260
take the national history, identity, or even appearance.

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:50.460
Transfer it to a rectangle and-- voila! A flag!

00:00:50.559 --> 00:00:55.439
But this means that similar countries conveying similar things can have similar flags.

00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:58.260
The Arab Revolt was a pretty big deal.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:01.349
Its flag became the inspiration for much of the middle East.

00:01:01.539 --> 00:01:08.518
Egypt, Iraq, Kuwait, Sudan, Syria, the UAE, Palestine, Yemen, Somaliland, and Libya chair a history and thus similar flags.

00:01:08.518 --> 00:01:09.670
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:12.930
POLYMATTER: Cyprus, a master of copy-paste, has no overlap to worry about.

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:14.670
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:18.019
POLYMATTER: With no restraints, flags could easily all be unique.

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:23.780
But countries often follow rules of design, and with everyone, the pool of possible flags [INAUDIBLE].

00:01:24.299 --> 00:01:29.599
Simplicity is a common recommendation, which makes for a pretty flag but not always a distinct one.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:34.369
Monaco, Indonesia, and Poland accidentally sacrificed originality for elegance.

00:01:34.979 --> 00:01:40.429
Other countries take inspiration from their neighbors. The story of the Nordic Cross goes something like this:

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:44.980
Denmark found this design and announced
"Hey look! We have a new flag!"

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:48.760
To which Sweden replied:
"How odd! We also have a new flag..."

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:52.880
Finland, Norway, and Iceland-- not wanting to feel left out, all nodded along.

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:53.420
And,

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:57.560
Greenland pretended not to hear. But perhaps, all this isn't so bad.

00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:00.259
If the rectangles which represent us are so similar,

00:02:00.619 --> 00:02:03.229
maybe we're all more alike than it seems.

00:02:03.229 --> 00:02:04.320
Or,

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.540
perhaps, we're just not very creative for their flags.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:14.960
Can someone create a purple flag? Just one, please? Just need one...

